-- title:
--   Étale on an open: stalk map carries maximal ideal onto maximal ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes in a fixed universe, $f \colon X \to Y$ a morphism of schemes, and $W$ an open subscheme of $X$ whose inclusion morphism $W.\iota$ composed with $f$ (that is, the restriction of $f$ to $W$) is étale. Let $x$ be a point of $X$ lying in $W$. The assertion is that the ideal of the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ generated by the image of the maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}_{Y,f(x)}$ of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{Y,f(x)}$ under the ring homomorphism $f^{\sharp}_x \colon \mathcal{O}_{Y,f(x)} \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ induced by $f$ on stalks at $x$ equals the maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}_{X,x}$. Equivalently, the stalk map of $f$ at $x$ is unramified at $x$ in the sense that it carries the maximal ideal of the target local ring onto, not merely into, the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$; note that étaleness is assumed only for the restriction of $f$ to the open neighbourhood $W$ of $x$, not for $f$ itself.
--
--   This is the standard local statement that an étale morphism is unramified, in the form $\mathfrak{m}_{Y,f(x)}\mathcal{O}_{X,x} = \mathfrak{m}_{X,x}$, with étaleness required only on an open neighbourhood of the point. It is used in the analysis of charts on models of modular curves at $p$, where it yields that the pull-back along an étale map of a uniformiser is again a uniformiser.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Hom_map_stalkMap_maximalIdeal_eq_of_etale_restrict.lean

